From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: Fix out-of-bounds frag access
 in Tx completion

Root-caused two live kernel panics hit tonight under sustained router
load (both "Unable to handle kernel paging request" with a
garbage-looking fault address, one during __netdev_alloc_skb() in the
RX NAPI path, one directly in edma_tx_complete() during the TX NAPI
path -- captured via pstore, symbolized precisely against this exact
build with scripts/faddr2line, not guessed at). Both point to the
same root cause, unrelated to the PPE hardware offload work landing
alongside this patch: edma_tx_complete()'s scatter-gather unmap path.

frag_index is derived from EDMA_TXCOMP_POOL_ID_GET(txcmpl) -- a value
read back from the hardware's own Tx completion descriptor -- and used
directly to index skb_shinfo(skb)->frags[] with no check that it
actually falls within that skb's own nr_frags at completion time.
There's a WARN_ON_ONCE(nr_frags > MAX_SKB_FRAGS) elsewhere in this
file, but that only validates the skb's own nr_frags at *submission*
time; nothing validates the hardware-reported value against it at
*completion* time. If a completion descriptor is ever stale or
mismatched with the skb it's paired with -- plausible under the kind
of sustained multi-stream load this was found under -- this reads an
out-of-bounds skb_frag_t and hands its garbage contents to
virt_to_phys() and dma_unmap_page() as if it were a real mapped page,
corrupting or directly crashing on whatever that garbage "physical
address" happens to be.

Add the missing bounds check: on mismatch, count it (new
invalid_frag_index stat, matching this function's existing
invalid_buffer/errors/desc_with_more_bit counters) and log it
ratelimited, but skip the unsafe dma_unmap_page() call rather than
follow a pointer we can't trust. The skb is still freed either way
(dev_kfree_skb() a few lines below, unconditionally) -- this only
skips the one unsafe unmap, accepting a rare leaked DMA mapping on the
anomaly path in exchange for not crashing on it.

Boot-tested and load-tested on ap2 (repeated single-stream and up to
8-way parallel iperf3 at ~1.8-1.9 Gbit/s) prior to this fix, where it
reproduced twice; verification that this specific fix prevents
recurrence has not yet been completed as of this commit.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.h
@@ -158,6 +158,8 @@
  * @errors: Other Tx complete descriptor errors indicated by the hardware.
  * @desc_with_more_bit: Packet's segment transmit count.
  * @no_pending_desc: No descriptor is pending for processing.
+ * @invalid_frag_index: Hardware-reported fragment pool ID didn't fit within
+ * the skb's own nr_frags at completion time.
  * @syncp: Synchronization pointer.
  */
 struct edma_txcmpl_stats {
@@ -165,6 +167,7 @@
 	u64 errors;
 	u64 desc_with_more_bit;
 	u64 no_pending_desc;
+	u64 invalid_frag_index;
 	struct u64_stats_sync syncp;
 };
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
@@ -173,10 +173,31 @@
 						 skb_headlen(skb), DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 			} else {
 				u8 frag_index = (EDMA_TXCOMP_POOL_ID_GET(txcmpl) - 1);
-				skb_frag_t *frag = &skb_shinfo(skb)->frags[frag_index];
 
-				dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(frag),
-					       PAGE_SIZE, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+				/* frag_index comes from the hardware's own
+				 * completion descriptor, not from the skb's
+				 * software state -- if a completion is ever
+				 * stale or mismatched with its skb, indexing
+				 * frags[] without this check reads an
+				 * out-of-bounds skb_frag_t and hands its
+				 * garbage contents to virt_to_phys() and
+				 * dma_unmap_page() as if it were a real page.
+				 */
+				if (unlikely(frag_index >= skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags)) {
+					if (net_ratelimit())
+						dev_warn(dev, "Invalid frag_index:%u nr_frags:%u cons_idx:%u prod_idx:%u\n",
+							 frag_index, skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags,
+							 cons_idx, prod_idx);
+
+					u64_stats_update_begin(&txcmpl_stats->syncp);
+					++txcmpl_stats->invalid_frag_index;
+					u64_stats_update_end(&txcmpl_stats->syncp);
+				} else {
+					skb_frag_t *frag = &skb_shinfo(skb)->frags[frag_index];
+
+					dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(frag),
+						       PAGE_SIZE, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+				}
 			}
 
 			dev_dbg(dev, "TXCMPL: skb:%pK, skb->len %d, skb->data_len %d, cons_idx:%d prod_idx:%d word2:0x%x word3:0x%x\n",
